# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Python environment. Never commit this.
#
# It is about 4 GB, and everything in it is compiled for one machine's Python
# and one machine's CUDA version. Copying it to the other laptop would produce
# a broken environment, not a working one. Rebuild it there with setup.ps1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Large sample media, re-downloadable from supremecourt.gov.
#
# These are public-domain government works and every source URL is recorded in
# asr-capstone/sample/manifest.json, so they can be fetched again on any
# machine with fetch_sample_media.py. Keeping 134 MB of audio out of the repo
# keeps clones fast, and git stores every future version of a binary file
# forever even after you delete it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.mp3
*.wav
*.flac
*.m4a

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The textbook. Copyrighted, so it must not go to a hosted repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Information theory inference and Learning algorithms.pdf

# The course syllabus. The professor's document, kept here to read, not to publish.
DATA 698 Fall 2026 Syllabus.docx

# The capstone proposal as submitted on 16 October 2026. Kept here to read.
/_Same Room, Same Problems_*.pdf

# Notes for conversations with the professor. Private, so they stay local.
asr-capstone/professor-meeting-2026-10-15.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model weights and downloaded corpora. These get very large and can always be
# fetched again from Hugging Face or the Court's site.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
models/
checkpoints/
# Anchored to the repository root on purpose. Written as a bare `corpus/` this
# matched any directory of that name at any depth, which silently swallowed the
# whole of `pipeline/scotus_asr/corpus/` - the source package that does the
# downloading. Five source files sat untracked and nothing reported it, because
# an ignored file is not an error. The downloaded corpus itself now lives
# outside the repository entirely.
/corpus/
data/raw/
*.ckpt
*.pt
*.bin
*.onnx
*.safetensors

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pipeline run output.
#
# Keep the findings, drop the intermediates. The result tables are small and are
# the point of the exercise, so they stay. The per-case JSON is large - a
# 150-minute transcription with a timestamp on every word - and can be produced
# again from the audio, so it does not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
asr-capstone/runs/*/asr/
asr-capstone/runs/*/reference/
asr-capstone/runs/*/ocr/
asr-capstone/runs/*/scores/
asr-capstone/runs/*/*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python leftovers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.pytest_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors and operating systems
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
*~
*.tmp
*.bak

# Local Claude Code settings are per-machine; the shared config is committed.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Exported notebooks. `nbconvert --to html` rebuilds one in seconds from the
# committed .ipynb, and it is the .ipynb that carries the work. Keeping the
# export out means the history does not collect a fresh 680 KB copy every time
# a cell is re-run. Regenerate one to email:
#   python -m nbconvert --to html notebooks/first-look.ipynb
# The leading ** matters: a pattern containing a slash is anchored to this
# file's own directory, so a bare notebooks/*.html would only match a
# notebooks/ folder at the repository root, not asr-capstone/notebooks/.
**/notebooks/*.html

# Proposal drafts: kept on this laptop only
asr-capstone/proposal/

# Where the corpora are on this laptop; each laptop makes its own from local-paths.example.yaml.
asr-capstone/local-paths.yaml

# Remade on demand: the invented-words cache (tools/find_invented_words.py), clips cut
# for the website (tools/flip_clips.py, tools/build_site.py) and browser-check screenshots.
asr-capstone/findings/invented-words/cache/
asr-capstone/findings/failures/cache/
asr-capstone/findings/failures/edits/restarts-dropped.csv.gz
asr-capstone/findings/failures/skips/cache/
asr-capstone/findings/failures/breakdown/cache/
asr-capstone/site/clips/
asr-capstone/site/checks/
asr-capstone/site/data/

# The recording explorer's data (tools/build_explorer.py): about 200 MB of transcripts,
# audio and copies of findings/, all rebuilt from the runs. Only its page is kept.
asr-capstone/explorer/*
!asr-capstone/explorer/*/
asr-capstone/explorer/*/*
!asr-capstone/explorer/*/index.html

# API keys: never committed. The judge reads the Claude key from this folder
# (tools/judge_failures.py, key_from_file); ANTHROPIC_API_KEY works too.
API keys/
**/API keys/
*.key
.env

# The LLM judge's full request logs (every request's timings and thinking text, several
# MB a model). A gzipped copy, requests-*.jsonl.gz, is committed instead; regzip after a run.
asr-capstone/findings/judge/requests-*.jsonl
# Batch bookkeeping (ids and what each request was): a working file, large on a full run.
asr-capstone/findings/judge/batch-*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Papers for the literature review. Copyrighted, so they stay local;
# asr-capstone/literature/pdf-index.md lists each one and where it came from,
# and asr-capstone/literature/fetch_papers.py downloads them again.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
asr-capstone/literature/pdfs/

# Drafts of the midterm paper, kept local while they are rough (1 Nov 2026).
asr-capstone/midterm/drafts/

# The public site (asr-capstone/tools/build_public.py): about 0.9 GB of pages, clips and
# audio, rebuilt from site/ and explorer/ and uploaded to Cloudflare Pages, never committed.
asr-capstone/public/

# Built from midterm/draft.md by midterm/make_pdf.py; rebuild instead of committing
asr-capstone/midterm/midterm-draft.pdf
asr-capstone/midterm/midterm-draft.html
